ILLUSTRATIVE C++:

struct KnowledgeQuery {
    GoalId goal;
    std::vector<ObjectId> knownPremises;
    std::vector<Constraint> constraints;
    std::size_t maxResults;
};

struct KnowledgeResult {
    std::vector<KnowledgeObject> objects;
    std::vector<Derivation> derivations;
    bool complete;
};

KnowledgeResult query(
    const KnowledgeQuery& request
);

The result-completeness flag is important.

A query interrupted by a timeout,
missing IPFS block or resource limit
must not be treated as evidence that
the requested knowledge does not exist.


============================================================
20. CHAIN OF KNOWLEDGE (CoK)
============================================================

The CoK is a persistent typed,
directed hypergraph.

It stores both knowledge and
the relationships that justify
derived conclusions.

OBJECT TYPES:

CLAIM
FACT
RULE
ASSUMPTION
HYPOTHESIS
DERIVATION
EVIDENCE
GOAL
ACTION
EXPERIMENT
TEST_RESULT
CONTRADICTION

RELATIONSHIP TYPES:

REQUIRES
IMPLIES
SUPPORTS
CONTRADICTS
DEPENDS_ON
PRODUCES
TESTS
ALTERNATIVE_TO

KNOWLEDGE STATES:

PROVISIONAL
VERIFIED
DISPUTED
INVALIDATED
UNRESOLVED
ARCHIVED

EACH DERIVATION STORES:

- Derivation ID
- Premise IDs
- Rule ID
- Conclusion ID
- Assumptions
- Evidence references
- Source provenance
- Verification result
- Dependency references
- Version metadata

The CoK is not merely a collection
of text embeddings.

It is a structured, queryable
knowledge and derivation system.


============================================================
21. KNOWLEDGE DSL
============================================================

Knowledge DSL is the canonical
machine-parsable representation
of structured knowledge.

PURPOSE:

- Persistent knowledge storage
- Deterministic parsing
- Versioned schemas
- Logical rule representation
- Evidence references
- Derivation reconstruction
- Cross-instance exchange
- Human inspection

OBJECT CATEGORIES:

ENTITY
CLAIM
RULE
ASSUMPTION
EVIDENCE
DERIVATION
CONTRADICTION
EXPERIMENT
TEST_RESULT

DESIRED PROPERTIES:

- Stable object identifiers
- Explicit type system
- Canonical serialisation
- Versioned grammar
- Reference validation
- Deterministic interpretation
- Provenance preservation

Knowledge DSL is distinct from
Task IR.

Task IR describes what the user
wants GammaThink to do.

Knowledge DSL describes what
GammaThink knows, assumes,
derives or has observed.


============================================================
22. ILLUSTRATIVE KNOWLEDGE DSL
============================================================

ENTITY sample_A {
    TYPE: water_sample
}

CLAIM C001 {
    SUBJECT: pure_water
    PROPERTY: freezing_point
    VALUE: 0
    UNIT: celsius
    CONDITIONS: {
        pressure: 1_atm
    }
    EVIDENCE: [E001]
    STATUS: VERIFIED
}

CLAIM C002 {
    SUBJECT: sample_A
    PROPERTY: temperature
    VALUE: -5
    UNIT: celsius
    EVIDENCE: [E002]
    STATUS: PROVISIONAL
}

ASSUMPTION A001 {
    SUBJECT: sample_A
    PROPERTY: composition
    VALUE: pure_water
}

ASSUMPTION A002 {
    SUBJECT: sample_A
    PROPERTY: pressure
    VALUE: 1_atm
}

RULE R001 {
    NAME: freezing_equilibrium
    PREMISES: [C001, C002, A001, A002]
    CONCLUSION: C003
}

CLAIM C003 {
    SUBJECT: sample_A
    PROPERTY: equilibrium_phase
    VALUE: solid
    STATUS: PROVISIONAL
}

DERIVATION D001 {
    PREMISES: [C001, C002, A001, A002]
    RULE: R001
    CONCLUSION: C003
    VERIFICATION: PENDING
}

This is illustrative syntax,
not the final executable grammar.

Physical conclusions depend on
the exact rule semantics and
applicable thermodynamic conditions.

The production DSL must represent
these conditions explicitly.


============================================================
23. DIRECTED HYPERGRAPH SEMANTICS
============================================================

A conventional graph edge connects
two nodes.

A hypergraph relationship can involve
multiple premises simultaneously.

EXAMPLE:

        CLAIM A       CLAIM B       CLAIM C
           |             |             |
           +-------------+-------------+
                         |
                         v
                  +-------------+
                  | RULE R17    |
                  | DERIVATION  |
                  | D42         |
                  +------+------+
                         |
                         v
                    CLAIM D

The conclusion depends on the
combined premises.

SUPPORTED STRUCTURES:

- Multi-premise derivations
- Shared dependencies
- Alternative proofs
- Contradictory claims
- Cross-domain inference
- Evidence chains
- Nested derivations

The underlying hypergraph is
a logical data structure.

It need not be spatially 3D.

A 2D or 3D layout may be used
for human visualisation.


